+### 0052 — BUG-260823-1vx45a: managerlock helper deadline conflated slowness with contention
+
+- ROOT CAUSE: `TestManagerLockHelper` used one 200ms context for lock-state directory creation, lock-file opening, retries, and, in build-key mode, two sequential acquisitions. Any deadline expiry was serialized as `blocked`, so an uncontended lock on a slow Windows runner was indistinguishable from a lock held by another process. Native Windows reproduction with a deliberately tiny deadline returned the same false `blocked` outcome without contention; no lock-handle or `t.TempDir` cleanup failure was involved.
+- FIX AND REGRESSION SHAPE: parent call sites now state whether they expect `blocked` or `acquired`. Blocked probes retain the bounded 200ms contention window, while expected-acquired probes receive a 30s deadline that has no fast-path cost. A deterministic subprocess regression gives an uncontended expected-acquired shape a 1ns deadline and requires `blocked`, while also asserting the acquired deadline remains strictly wider than the blocked deadline. Helper lock release errors are checked independently before acquisition errors are classified, so a failed close cannot be reported as ordinary contention. The separate 50ms timeout in `TestCancellationReleasesPartialAcquisition` remains intentionally short because the test holds the second lock and expects cancellation; host slowness cannot turn that negative assertion into a false acquisition.
+- CURRENT-BYTE EVIDENCE: macOS subprocess tests passed 20 consecutive runs, the managerlock package passed 5 consecutive runs, and the subprocess race gate passed 3 consecutive runs. Linux/arm64 Go 1.25.5 and native Windows/amd64 Go 1.25.5 each passed all `TestSubprocess*` cases 20 consecutive times. `go vet ./...`, `golangci-lint run`, and `go build ./...` exited 0. A broad `go test ./...` produced no package output and was terminated at approximately seven minutes before the single-call ceiling (exit 143); it is recorded as interrupted, not passing, and the scope-specific package and platform gates are the acceptance evidence.
